WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.730
A payments ledger is 2 terabytes of 8 kilobyte pages on an SSD, about 100 microseconds a read.

2
00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:17.284
So the database keeps copies of pages in a fixed set of slots in RAM, called frames: the buffer pool.

3
00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:19.228
A query asks for page 10.

4
00:00:19.228 --> 00:00:25.617
It isn't in the pool, so that's a miss: the pool reads it from the SSD into a free frame.

5
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.976
More misses fill the other frames.

6
00:00:27.976 --> 00:00:30.919
Ask for page 12 again, and it's already there.

7
00:00:30.919 --> 00:00:33.415
That's a hit, with no disk read at all.

8
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.415
A query reading page 11 right now holds a pin on it.

9
00:00:37.415 --> 00:00:40.056
A frame with a pin is never evicted.

10
00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:43.221
An update changed page 13 in memory only.

11
00:00:43.221 --> 00:00:46.680
Its frame is dirty: newer than the copy on disk.

12
00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:50.133
Now page 50 is needed, and every frame is full.

13
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:51.817
Which page should leave?

14
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.048
Evicting the page loaded first would throw out page 10, the top of an index that every query reads.

15
00:00:58.048 --> 00:01:00.675
The next query would read it straight back.

16
00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:05.758
Least recently used is better, but it reorders one shared list on every hit.

17
00:01:05.758 --> 00:01:10.971
CLOCK keeps one bit per frame instead: a hit sets its frame's reference bit to 1.

18
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:14.742
To find a victim, a hand sweeps the frames like a clock.

19
00:01:14.742 --> 00:01:18.801
A bit set to 1 is cleared, and that frame gets a second chance.

20
00:01:18.801 --> 00:01:20.476
A pinned frame is skipped.

21
00:01:21.433 --> 00:01:26.283
The first unpinned frame with its bit at 0 is the victim: page 13.

22
00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:31.076
Page 13 is dirty, so the pool writes it back to the SSD first.

23
00:01:31.076 --> 00:01:33.157
A clean one is just dropped.

24
00:01:33.367 --> 00:01:38.024
Then page 50 is read into that frame, and the hand rests just past it.

25
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.890
A hot page sets its bit again before the hand comes back, so it stays.

26
00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:45.101
A page touched once leaves first.

27
00:01:45.101 --> 00:01:49.121
PostgreSQL uses a small count, up to 5, in place of the bit.

28
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.809
A buffer pool keeps hot pages in a fixed set of frames.

29
00:01:52.809 --> 00:01:58.999
Never evict a pinned page, give recent ones a second chance, and write a dirty victim back first.
